I’m testing one new residual-tail lever after all: an RDNA4-only DPP reduction for the four within-row reduction steps, while retaining the initial lane-16 shuffle. This preserves the existing balanced accumulation order at lane 0, keeps the same loads/FMA math, adds no persistent state, and attacks the serial tail rather than the already-hidden memory latency. The target source is copied to `/tmp` only; the shared dirty file is left untouched.
